false] [17592186425407 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Find $\\,\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C)).$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535495 17592186045443 true] [17592186425728 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139535495 17592186045443 false] [17592186425728 1074 "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 3t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139535495 17592186045443 true] [17592186425949 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -k+5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2y=3 \\\\ -(k-5)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=5,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+2y=3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=5.$" 13194139535495 17592186045443 false] [17592186425949 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -(k+1) & -9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -k+5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2y=3 \\\\ -(k-5)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=5,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+2y=3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=5.$" 13194139535495 17592186045443 true] [17592186426076 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$  is  invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$" 13194139535495 17592186045443 false] [17592186426076 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\nI. $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0$\n\nII. $A$  is  invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$" 13194139535495 17